--- !u!114 &1217780603
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1217780601}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 4008b5b6b93a72f4fb1bba633a47f5be, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  cameraMovement: {fileID: 330585548}
  journal: {fileID: 1956118639}
  bookHint: {fileID: 1882587698}
  sjoelHint: {fileID: 1784852410}
  koekhapHint: {fileID: 750270956}
  giveCubeHint: {fileID: 1551648909}
  funFactText: {fileID: 413394836}
  funFacts:
  - "King's Day (Koningsdag) is celebrated on April 27th, marking the birthday of
    King Willem-Alexander. It\u2019s the most festive national holiday in the Netherlands,
    with nearly everyone dressed in orange, the national color that honors the royal
    family\u2014the House of Orange-Nassau."
  - One of the most unique traditions is the vrijmarkt (free market), where people
    of all ages set up stalls to sell old clothes, books, toys, and homemade treats.
    It turns cities, towns, and even parks into massive open-air flea markets, reflecting
    the Dutch love for recycling and bargain-hunting.
  - "In cities like Amsterdam, the celebrations extend to the canals, where people
    throw lively boat parties, filling the waterways with music, dancing, and crowds
    dressed head-to-toe in orange. The whole city feels like a mix between a parade
    and a music festival\u2014but floating!"
  - "Orange madness (oranjegekte) isn\u2019t just about clothes\u2014people wear
    wigs, paint their faces, decorate bikes, and even dye their food and drinks orange.
    You'll find everything from orange pastries to orange beer, especially the popular
    tompouce, a bright orange cream-filled treat."
  - "The night before, called King\u2019s Night (Koningsnacht), is a full-blown party
    too\u2014cities like The Hague and Utrecht host huge outdoor concerts and DJ
    sets, drawing massive crowds before the main event even begins."
  - "Children play a big role too\u2014many get involved by selling toys or performing
    music and dance in the vrijmarkt, turning the streets into a kid-powered talent
    show and mini economy."
  - Some cities host quirky traditional games like spijkerpoepen (trying to lower
    a nail on a string into a bottle without using your hands) or koekhappen (catching
    a piece of cake dangling from a string in your mouth, blindfolded).
  endOfGame: {fileID: 409912184}
  inMinigame: 0
